{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he told his father</b> — He told this dream to his father and not the first one, for he himself recognized in its interpretation that the sun alludes to his father, and he rebuked him. And the sense of \"and to his brothers\" a second time is that he told it to his father in the presence of his brothers, and he rebuked him to appease their wrath against him. And the sense of \"What is this dream that you have dreamed?\" is like \"What is man that You should know him?\" (Psalms 144:3) — that is, what is this dream, it is nothing, that you should tell it, for they are but idle things. Or the sense of the rebuke is to say: how did your heart carry you to dream this dream? This is nothing but haughtiness and youthful fancy, that such things should rise upon your heart, as it is said concerning dreams (Daniel 2:29), \"your thoughts arose upon your bed,\" and it is written (Daniel 4:2), \"and the reflections upon my bed.\"" "<b>Shall we indeed come, I and your mother</b> — Now his mother was already dead, and he did not know that the matter extended to Bilhah, who raised him as a mother. And our Sages (Berakhot 55a) learned from here that no dream is without idle elements. And Jacob intended to remove the matter from the hearts of his sons so they would not be jealous of him on account of it; he said to him: just as it is impossible regarding your mother, so the whole thing is void — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 37:10). But in my opinion, at the time Jacob went down to Egypt, Bilhah had already died, as had Zilpah, for Scripture says in the account of the seventy souls (Genesis 46:26), \"apart from the wives of Jacob's sons,\" and it did not say \"apart from his wives and the wives of his sons.\" And if you should say that because they were concubines he did not wish to say \"apart from the wives of Jacob's sons and his concubines,\" behold, it is written \"his father's wives.\" And it is far-fetched that the moon should allude to his concubine. Rather, the substance of the dream is that the sun alludes to Jacob and the moon alludes to the members of his household and all his wives through whom his descendants came, and it alludes that all his descendants would bow down to him — they are all seventy souls issuing from his thigh, for all of them bowed down when they came to him, and these eleven stars are his brothers who bowed down to him by themselves before their father came, as it is written (Genesis 43:26), \"And Joseph came into the house, and they brought him the present that was in their hand into the house, and bowed down to him to the earth.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Daniel 2:29 and 4:2 in Aramaic; the Hebrew equivalents are rendered in English.", 1 "Ramban's citation of Genesis 46:26 reads 'apart from the wives of Jacob's sons' (מִלְּבַד נְשֵׁי בְנֵי יַעֲקֹב); his quotation appears to conflate verse 26 with the wording of verse 27 in some traditions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:14.459773883Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}